WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.853 --> 00:00:02.966
An order travels through the whole plant,

2
00:00:03.082 --> 00:00:06.426
from the ERP in the office to the machine on the floor.

3
00:00:07.103 --> 00:00:09.611
Here we look more closely at one boundary:

4
00:00:09.831 --> 00:00:12.304
the one between level four and level three,

5
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:15.033
between the ERP and the MES.

6
00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:19.318
ISA-95 describes two main flows there.

7
00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:23.397
Down goes the production schedule: what must be made.

8
00:00:24.111 --> 00:00:27.966
Up goes the production performance: what was really made and used.

9
00:00:28.709 --> 00:00:31.472
In short: orders down, confirmations up.

10
00:00:32.497 --> 00:00:37.293
Master data also goes down, such as bills of materials and routings.

11
00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:40.759
And sometimes an overview of the capacity goes up,

12
00:00:40.852 --> 00:00:43.801
so the planner knows what the plant can still take on.

13
00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:45.966
First, down.

14
00:00:46.695 --> 00:00:51.745
The ERP sends the production order to the MES: 1,000 caps,

15
00:00:51.978 --> 00:00:57.005
with 25 grams of granulate per cap according to the bill of materials.

16
00:00:57.734 --> 00:01:00.265
That is the plan: what, how many and when.

17
00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:07.490
The MES turns it into a work order: the job for one line or machine, in one shift.

18
00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.935
Here that is machine four, on the early shift.

19
00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.926
And the MES puts the work orders in the right sequence.

20
00:01:16.614 --> 00:01:20.480
For example the white caps first, then grey, then black,

21
00:01:20.596 --> 00:01:23.858
so the machine needs less purging at each colour change.

22
00:01:25.215 --> 00:01:26.689
Then the order starts.

23
00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:32.653
The MES, or the operator on the HMI, sends the recipe to the PLC.

24
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.740
A recipe is the set of settings for that one product: the temperatures,

25
00:01:37.984 --> 00:01:39.575
the speeds and the times.

26
00:01:40.318 --> 00:01:42.466
Every product has its own recipe.

27
00:01:43.423 --> 00:01:46.198
That way nobody has to type them in by hand.

28
00:01:46.954 --> 00:01:51.900
It saves time, and a typo in a temperature can easily cost a bin of scrap.

29
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:53.918
Now, up.

30
00:01:54.638 --> 00:01:56.635
The PLC counts every cycle.

31
00:01:57.326 --> 00:02:02.388
The MES collects the counts, the scrap with its reasons, and the downtime.

32
00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:04.749
What is good and what is scrap,

33
00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:10.194
only the shop floor knows: the ERP sees it only when the MES reports it.

34
00:02:11.278 --> 00:02:13.031
At the end of the order or the shift,

35
00:02:13.263 --> 00:02:19.312
the MES sends a confirmation to the ERP: so many good, so many scrap, so many hours.

36
00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:25.209
For this order the MES counted 960 good and 40 scrap.

37
00:02:25.942 --> 00:02:32.258
But in this example it confirms only the 960 good caps: the scrap is left out.

38
00:02:33.086 --> 00:02:34.259
Keep that in mind.

39
00:02:35.595 --> 00:02:41.493
With it, the ERP books a goods receipt: the 960 good caps go into stock.

40
00:02:42.261 --> 00:02:43.399
The scrap does not.

41
00:02:44.448 --> 00:02:46.689
What it is called depends on the system.

42
00:02:47.452 --> 00:02:53.059
In SAP, for example, the goods receipt for an order is movement type 101.

43
00:02:53.758 --> 00:02:58.541
The material used is booked out with movement type 261:

44
00:02:58.762 --> 00:03:00.434
the goods issue to the order.

45
00:03:01.437 --> 00:03:03.364
Other systems use other codes,

46
00:03:03.597 --> 00:03:08.844
but the idea is the same everywhere: the product comes in, the material goes out.

47
00:03:10.172 --> 00:03:12.041
But how does that material go out?

48
00:03:12.837 --> 00:03:17.411
Many companies use backflush: the ERP books it out automatically,

49
00:03:17.539 --> 00:03:20.534
from the bill of materials and the confirmed quantity.

50
00:03:21.527 --> 00:03:25.707
So nobody has to scan every bag of granulate that goes to the machine.

51
00:03:26.355 --> 00:03:28.526
That saves a lot of manual work.

52
00:03:29.253 --> 00:03:34.512
So one confirmation often sets off two bookings: the caps in and the granulate out.

53
00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:39.488
But backflush calculates with the standard and with what was confirmed.

54
00:03:40.305 --> 00:03:43.474
If scrap is higher than the bill of materials assumes,

55
00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:47.446
or only the good quantity counts, the stock is no longer right.

56
00:03:48.238 --> 00:03:51.129
In SAP, confirmed scrap is normally included;

57
00:03:51.373 --> 00:03:54.368
the gap then comes from scrap that is never confirmed.

58
00:03:55.775 --> 00:03:57.319
Work it out for this order.

59
00:03:58.043 --> 00:04:00.876
The MES confirmed only the good parts,

60
00:04:01.004 --> 00:04:07.784
so backflush books out 960 times 25 grams: 24 kilos of granulate.

61
00:04:08.812 --> 00:04:14.675
But what was really used is 1,000 times 25 grams: 25 kilos.

62
00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:18.074
The 40 scrapped caps took granulate too.

63
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.963
So after this one order, the ERP shows 1 kilo of granulate too much.

64
00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:29.151
A few months on, you hear: according to SAP we have three bags left,

65
00:04:29.301 --> 00:04:31.135
but there is nothing in the warehouse.

66
00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:37.053
The fix: confirm the scrap too, so backflush calculates with the total quantity,

67
00:04:37.238 --> 00:04:38.260
good plus scrap.

68
00:04:38.972 --> 00:04:41.538
Or book the scrap separately as consumption.

69
00:04:42.843 --> 00:04:46.245
In practice you hear: stock in SAP is never right.

70
00:04:46.976 --> 00:04:50.412
Often the cause is not in the warehouse, but in the confirmation.

71
00:04:51.436 --> 00:04:52.643
It comes too late.

72
00:04:53.422 --> 00:04:58.879
It is in the wrong unit, for example in pieces where the ERP expects boxes.

73
00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:01.154
Or the scrap is left out.

74
00:05:01.939 --> 00:05:04.551
Each of the three makes the stock in the ERP

75
00:05:04.586 --> 00:05:06.978
slowly drift away from what is really there.

76
00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:10.986
So ask the sharp question: how often do we confirm,

77
00:05:11.079 --> 00:05:12.937
per part, per shift or per order?

78
00:05:13.703 --> 00:05:15.236
And is scrap confirmed too?

79
00:05:16.243 --> 00:05:20.933
Per part is usually too much for the ERP; per week, too little for the planner.

80
00:05:21.619 --> 00:05:26.774
Orders down, confirmations up: when the way back is right, the stock is right too.
